hi there steve kaufman here again at  lang fest in montreal and i have with me  lydia machova  a friend from bratislava  where she organized with a group of  volunteers of course the bratislava  polyglot gathering and lydia is not only  a polyglot  and a learner of languages but she is  also involved in helping people learn  and i have a number of things i want to  ask you about but i want to start with  that and and of course you're presenting  on that subject here and so what exactly  is this idea of  helping people learn yeah  well it's my it's my own approach to  teaching languages i call it language  mentoring uh because i don't want to  come i don't want people to come to me  and expect to be taught a language that  i know i have the knowledge of and i  just impart the knowledge upon them i  don't think that works uh what i want  them to do is to find their own way how  they can learn a language how they can  spend enough time with the language not  just come to the lessons twice a week  for one hour and a half and to really  learn by themselves and i'll be i'll be  happy to help them with that i'm happy  to show them the resources and the  methods how to work with that but they  need to really do the work okay now  spend enough time on their own i  remember i was at a conference in  germany where  uh  and they said the average corporate  language learner in germany they did a  survey has one hour a week with a  teacher and the average learner puts in  one more hour  that's not very much that's not enough  what has been your experience with the  people that you help yeah uh my  experience that people usually think  they don't have enough time so they go  for the courses with the teacher because  they think oh i don't have enough i  don't have more time anyway but then i  show them ways how they can have fun  with the language and it's not really  seen as learning it's like well you're  reading a book that you love that you  loved in uh reading in another language  so you're just spending time with the  language and suddenly it's not seen as  oh i have to do this this is homework  but oh where can i when can i continue  to read my favorite book but let's  assume read favorite book okay  it's difficult to read a book in another  language yes well there's a few methods  how you can approach that first of all  what you can do is you either get a  bilingual book where you have it one  page and another page right or you get  two copies of the same book which is  something i like to do so i would get  the spanish harry potter and the slovak  harry potter and i would read and if i  cannot find if i don't understand  something and i mean not just one word  some part where i have no clue what  they're talking about i would look up  the slovak part right  uh or you can um  yeah you can go through the the text and  look at words that you don't know for  example through link right so that's  another way how you can work with text  but the thing is you really need to go  through a lot of material it's not  enough if you just read text in a  textbook at a language school right this  i think this will never be enough right  so and and then you help people uh  show them where there are resources show  them how to go about becoming  independent learners and are you able to  convert these people into independent  learners yes and i'm very very happy  about it yeah the first time i did this  uh this experiment because i didn't know  if it would work for other people too i  know it works for me  but i did an experiment with a hundred  students at a university uh they were  language students but they did not  really learn a lot by themselves i mean  the usual approach of a student who  studies to be a translator or  interpreter is oh i go to study to this  university and the university gives me  the language kind of right and i told  them guys let's not do it let's let's  spend a lot of free time with the  language let's watch series let's read  books let's uh you know listen to  podcasts as much as possible we  monitored it in some way and motivated  each other and suddenly they improved  immensely within two months so that's  where i tested it right and now i do it  for people who have nothing to do with  languages like they're not studying it  they don't necessarily like the foreign  languages they don't have a passion for  it but they they develop the passion  right this is so important and so we can  develop  people  the  passions  who tell me they hated english they  hated it for 20 years and they could not  progress suddenly fall in love with one  method one way of working with uh with  the language suddenly they can't stop  right and they spend  people who are working have families a  full-time job whatever spend uh 10 to 15  hours a week with the language and i  have this experience with hundreds of  people now in slovakia so it's  impossible and if you spend that much  time with the language you are going to  improve yes yes and they some might  improve faster than others but they will  all improve and i what i really liked in  your book by the way was you said you  are the best judge of how much you've  improved you don't need a test you don't  have someone to tell you and i honestly  think it's really impossible to tell if  you're now on a b2 or a b1 based on one  test which takes you half an hour right  like it's a just a choice of selection  of the vocabulary right so we agree that  you are the best judge yes because all  of a sudden you understand something  that you didn't understand three months  earlier which is a wonderful feeling it  is and you don't need to take a test  which you might pass and might fail  based on some arbitrary uh you know uh  criteria and you are primarily like me  an input based learner yes and the  question that always comes up within  that we input base learners are asked uh  two two questions really one how do you  then activate it  okay you've got all this input that's  gone in how do you get it to start  coming out and then the second question  is because it's easy to do let's say two  three months of input with language a  put language a aside and start in the  language b  and now there's a possibility of  interference between these two languages  especially if they're related languages  and i think you recently had some  experience with that so  two questions a how do you activate and  b how do you deal with interference yes  well i personally like to start speaking  in a language about two or three months  after i started with the input but this  is for languages that are close to the  languages that i already know i've never  started learning an asian language or  something very uh very far from my  languages so i think i would need more  time in that case but i'm not in a hurry  i don't need to start speaking right  away right i start speaking when i have  some vocabulary and it's not yet a  moment where i would be comfortable  speaking because it takes time but i  think well what people that are learning  their  ex language  have as an advantage is you know it's  going to take time and you're going to  improve we have patience absolutely and  we know we're going to get there you can  even in a way in a weird way you can  enjoy this frustration you know i have  to interrupt just to stress that  even at the beginning when we're  struggling  we know we're going to get there because  we've been in that struggling mode  forget everything no traction getting  nowhere and and it all sounds the same  and we know we're going to get there so  that's a big advantage that we have over  the inexperienced learner and then later  on we know it's a long road to get to  that comfortable fluency level and we're  quite happy to stay there and we're  enjoying it you're not expecting very  rapid improvement within one week and  you're not frustrated oh my god i still  can't speak exactly all right  and and we also know that it will  activate i mean that has been your  experience absolutely  and the best way actually what i do what  i like to do is so i i have some  speaking practice but it may be just two  lessons a week not like very very  intense and then i like to go to the  country and in two weeks i feel like i  could dream i could do anything in there  it's such a freedom i know and that was  my feeling unfortunately i only had like  six days in prague so it wasn't as great  as it could have been i had three weeks  in la vie for ukrainian and one week is  not enough it's two to three weeks  to activate and boom  okay second question you know and i i  mentioned that i had put a lot of effort  into slovak leading up to the uh  conference in bratislava and even though  i had spent so much more time on  ukrainian i met a ukrainian or there  were several ukrainians in batista i  couldn't say a thing yes and yet  i would say half an hour into a  conversation with with solomia in la  life my ukrainian was back that quickly  i have the same experience okay so talk  a little bit about this interference  thing and is it a problem is it  something we should worry about yeah or  or we just accept the fact sometimes we  just can't perform you know that's the  answer i mean i used to worry about that  i was i was hesitating but i want to  start with russian because my polish is  pretty pretty good and pretty uh pretty  advanced i didn't want to lose it  because i've put so much time into it  right so what i did now i did learn  russian because i wanted to travel in  both soviet countries and exactly this  is my experience what happened i was in  russia i took the trans-siberian express  from moscow to mongolia speaking just  russian now i met three polish guys in  uh at the end of my trip i couldn't say  a word and  for the at the beginning i was a bit  scared it's like oh my god i've lost my  polish this is terrible you know like i  i sacrificed it to russian but then i  put russian aside and i concentrated  just on polish and it took me about a  day to gain my full fluency again but  what i what i found out i cannot do is  when i was on the phone with a polish  guy with russians sitting next to me and  they would they would go silent when  they heard me speaking in islamic  language which was not russian and and  they would uh try to translate what i  was saying in polish into russian and i  was on the phone and i couldn't say  anything in any language i was totally  lost for words because these two  languages were coming in at the same  time the same content  you know and simultaneously i couldn't  do it even though i'm a trained  interpreter you know you know and and  we'll sort of close on that on that note  it's of course the brain the brain  learns the brain cannot help but learn  yes exposed to experiences the brain is  going to develop some kind of neural uh  connections uh but sometimes we can't  control it and i find that at times i'll  be speaking  i don't know russian or or when i'm  struggling let's say not russian maybe  it's ukrainian maybe it's slovak and out  comes japanese  yes wow like the i don't know what  happened up there but they kind of took  the wrong turn and when did these things  happen yes we can't control the brain i  think we just need to be confident the  brain will learn  uh it's not perfect  occasionally it will let us down yes but  by and large it does a good job exactly  okay well thank you  all right thank you very much  [Music]  you

